def L(i,t,q,s,m,a,tr,f):return {'id':f'ANS-{i:03d}','topic':t,'question':q,'shortAnswer':s,'modelAnswer':m,'assumptions':a,'traps':tr,'followUps':f,'reviewedOn':D,'status':'reviewed-full-v1'}
B=[
L(276,'Reactor Physics','What is neutron multiplication?','Neutron multiplication describes how one generation produces the next after fission production, absorption and leakage; k_eff is their population ratio.','If k_eff is below one the chain decreases, equal to one it is steady and above one it grows in the modeled state. The value depends on composition, geometry, spectrum, leakage and feedback. Power dynamics also require delayed neutrons and thermal/control response. This is conceptual physics only; operation belongs to authorized facilities.', ['Defined multiplying configuration','Generation-averaged model'],['Equating multiplication with explosion','Ignoring leakage/absorption'],['Define k_eff.','Why delayed neutrons?','Moderator role?','What is feedback?','Why critical can be normal?']),
L(277,'Radiation Protection','Why is monitoring important?','Monitoring provides evidence about radiation fields, contamination, dose-related quantities and abnormal change so controls and authorized decisions can be verified.','Area, personal and contamination monitoring measure different quantities with calibrated instruments and defined response. Results require background, geometry, energy response, limits, uncertainty and records. Monitoring does not replace source control or procedure, and a zero reading only supports absence above the instrument’s sensitivity. Alarm settings and actions come from facility rules.', ['Authorized programme and calibrated monitor','Quantity and detection limit specified'],['Calling no reading no radiation','Using wrong monitor for the hazard'],['Area versus personal?','What is background?','Why energy response?','What if alarm?','How record trend?']),
L(278,'Radiation Detectors','What is a Geiger counter useful for?','A Geiger–Müller counter is useful for robust detection and survey counting of ionizing events, but usually provides little energy information and has dead-time/rate limitations.','A gas avalanche spreads along the tube, producing nearly uniform pulse size independent of initial deposited energy. It can locate elevated fields or contamination when the probe/window and radiation are suitable. Efficiency, geometry, background, plateau voltage, dead time and saturation matter. Counts are not automatically activity or dose.', ['Suitable GM tube/probe and radiation type','Authorized survey/calibration context'],['Calling count rate dose','Using GM for spectroscopy'],['Why no energy resolution?','What is dead time?','Plateau meaning?','Window effect?','How background enters?']),
L(279,'Radiation Detectors','Why use semiconductor detectors?','Semiconductors convert deposited energy into many electron–hole pairs with low creation energy, enabling compact detectors and often good energy resolution.','A depleted junction collects carriers to form a pulse proportional to deposited energy when charge collection is complete. Material band gap, atomic number, thickness, bias, leakage, capacitance, trapping, temperature and radiation damage shape efficiency and resolution. Cooling may reduce leakage. They are not universally best: scintillators can offer larger volume, speed or cost advantages.', ['Detector material/geometry and radiation specified','Bias and readout calibrated'],['Saying semiconductors detect every radiation efficiently','Ignoring charge trapping/leakage'],['Why good resolution?','What sets efficiency?','Why cooling?','Compare scintillator.','Radiation damage?']),
L(280,'Radiation Instrumentation','What determines detector energy resolution?','Energy resolution combines statistical carrier/photon production, collection nonuniformity, sensor gain, electronic noise, calibration, material effects and event processing.','It is often quoted as FWHM at a specified energy or ΔE/E. Fano/statistical variation sets a lower bound, while leakage, capacitance, optical collection, trapping, ballistic deficit, pile-up and temperature add width. The observed peak also includes source/environment effects. Resolution must be measured with suitable lines and stable calibration, not inferred from channel count.', ['Specified detector and energy','Peak model/calibration defined'],['Equating ADC bins with resolution','Quoting resolution without energy'],['FWHM meaning?','Fano factor?','Electronic noise?','Pile-up?','How calibrate width?']),
L(281,'Measurement Science','How do you distinguish signal from noise?','Define expected signal structure and noise model, use controls and matched conditions, quantify SNR/detection performance and test reproducibility on independent data.','Signal can be distinguished by known time, spectrum, spatial pattern, modulation, scaling or response to a controlled variable. Noise may be stochastic, drift, interference or model mismatch. I would inspect raw/time/frequency data, backgrounds and residuals, then use a predeclared detector and false-alarm criterion. Filtering alone cannot prove the remaining feature is signal.', ['Signal mechanism and observation window defined','Representative controls/background'],['Calling any repeatable artifact signal','Smoothing into expected shape'],['What is SNR?','Which control?','How false alarm measured?','What modulation helps?','When insufficient evidence?']),
L(282,'Radiation Effects in Materials','How does radiation affect materials?','Radiation deposits energy and displaces atoms, creating excitation, ionization, defects, transmutation or microstructural change that can alter mechanical, electrical, optical and chemical properties.','Effects depend on particle/photon type, energy, dose/fluence/rate, temperature, material and environment. Point defects can migrate, recombine, cluster or interact with interfaces; ionizing damage changes dielectrics/electronics. Testing needs authorized irradiation, dosimetry, controls, before/after characterization and safe handling. A generic radiation exposure cannot predict one property direction.', ['Radiation spectrum and exposure quantity defined','Authorized facility and material condition'],['Equating dose and fluence','Assuming all defects accumulate linearly'],['Ionization versus displacement?','Dose-rate effect?','Annealing?','Which characterization?','How control temperature?']),
L(283,'Nuclear Materials','Why are defects important?','Defects govern diffusion, trapping, swelling, embrittlement, conductivity, corrosion and phase evolution; irradiation continually creates and redistributes them.','Vacancies, interstitials, dislocations, interfaces and precipitates act as sources, sinks and transport paths. Their effect depends on concentration, mobility, temperature, stress and chemistry. Controlled microstructure can improve tolerance, while clusters or segregation can degrade it. Evidence requires multiple scales and comparison with unirradiated/control material.', ['Defect type and property defined','Temperature/exposure history known'],['Calling all defects damage','Inferring mechanism from one bulk property'],['What is defect sink?','How temperature matters?','Why grain boundaries?','How measure defects?','What control?']),
L(284,'Nuclear Materials','How would you characterize an irradiated material within an authorized facility?','Follow authorization and chain of custody, establish exposure/dose and controls, then use approved structural, compositional, microstructural and property methods with contamination and uncertainty controls.','I would not transport or prepare material outside procedure. Before/after and unirradiated references help distinguish irradiation from batch/handling effects. XRD, microscopy, spectroscopy, dimensional/mechanical/thermal/electrical tests may be selected by hypothesis and activity constraints. Sampling, shielding, remote handling, waste and release criteria are facility-controlled. Results must separate measured changes from inferred mechanisms.', ['Authorized radiological facility/procedure','Exposure and sample history traceable'],['Improvising sample handling','Calling one change radiation mechanism proof'],['Which control?','How record dose?','What method first?','How test contamination?','What result falsifies mechanism?']),
L(285,'Nuclear Materials','What properties matter in nuclear materials?','Relevant properties depend on function but may include neutron interaction, phase/chemical stability, thermal transport, mechanical integrity, corrosion, swelling, creep, radiation damage and inspectability.','Fuel, cladding, moderator, coolant-facing, shielding and detector materials have different requirements. Temperature gradients, stress, chemistry and irradiation interact over lifetime. A useful answer begins from component function and failure criteria, then connects properties to qualified measurements and models. No material is “nuclear grade” from composition or one room-temperature test alone.', ['Component/function and environment specified','Public conceptual scope'],['Listing properties without role','Ignoring coupled degradation'],['Which component?','Dominant failure?','How test corrosion?','Why thermal conductivity?','What surveillance evidence?']),
L(286,'Radiation Safety','How do you respond to a suspected contamination event?','Stop work, avoid spreading or touching material, warn/secure the area as trained, notify authorized radiation-protection staff and follow the facility procedure.','I would not attempt cleanup or use an arbitrary survey method. Qualified personnel determine monitoring, boundaries, decontamination, personal assessment, waste and reporting. I would preserve event details—time, location, source/work, people and actions—without compromising safety. Contamination is material, so response differs from simply leaving an external radiation field.', ['Actual facility procedure controls response','No operational cleanup advice'],['Cleaning before monitoring','Leaving without reporting'],['Contamination versus irradiation?','Why avoid spread?','Who monitors?','What record?','When resume work?']),
L(287,'Research Safety','Why must experiments be documented?','Documentation provides traceability for safety, configuration, calibration, data integrity, reproducibility, anomalies and accountability.','A contemporaneous record links objective, sample, equipment, settings, authorization, hazards, raw files, code, deviations and results. It lets others reconstruct work, investigate abnormal outcomes and determine whether controls were followed. In nuclear or mission work, undocumented configuration can invalidate both science and safety case. Records should preserve corrections and access controls rather than be rewritten after success.', ['Institutional records policy followed','Configuration/identifiers defined'],['Documenting only final plots','Using confidentiality to avoid authorized records'],['What before run?','How note deviation?','Why raw data path?','Who reviews?','How protect confidential records?']),
L(288,'Scientific Reporting','How would you report an abnormal result?','Make the system safe, preserve raw evidence/configuration, report promptly through the proper channel, separate observation from interpretation and propose a controlled investigation.','I would state what requirement or baseline was violated, timing, conditions, uncertainty and immediate actions. I would not hide, delete or “fix” the value before review. Calibration, sample identity, instrument health, interfaces and procedure deviations are checked, with safe reproduction only if authorized. Impact and next steps are updated as evidence develops.', ['Reporting/escalation channel exists','Safe state achieved first'],['Waiting until root cause is known','Blaming operator or instrument without evidence'],['What is observation?','When stop work?','What evidence preserve?','How reproduce safely?','How communicate uncertainty?']),
L(289,'Radar Systems','How is target range measured by a pulsed radar, and what limits maximum detectable range?','Range comes from round-trip delay R=cΔt/2; maximum detectable range follows whether received echo energy exceeds noise/clutter and detection requirements after propagation and system losses.','A known pulse or compressed waveform is transmitted and delay estimated by matched processing. Received power in an ideal monostatic radar falls strongly with range and depends on transmit power, gains, wavelength, target scattering and losses. Noise temperature, bandwidth, integration, false-alarm threshold, clutter, atmosphere, ambiguity, duty cycle and hardware dynamic range set practical range. Maximum range is not range resolution or unambiguous range.', ['Public generic monostatic model','Waveform, target/statistics and detection criterion defined'],['Using delay formula as maximum range','Confusing resolution and detectability'],['Radar equation factors?','Bandwidth and resolution?','What is unambiguous range?','How integration helps?','How false alarm enters?']),
L(290,'Transistors','Why bias a transistor?','Biasing establishes a quiescent operating point that provides intended gain, headroom, linearity and stability before the signal is applied.','For a BJT or MOSFET, device parameters and temperature vary, so a bias network and often feedback stabilize current/voltage. The load line and supply determine cutoff/saturation limits and signal swing. Poor bias causes distortion, clipping, excess power or sensitivity. Bias does not guarantee high-frequency stability, and small-signal models apply only around the operating point.', ['Specific transistor topology and supply','Small-signal operation around Q point'],['Saying bias only turns transistor on','Ignoring temperature/device spread'],['What is Q point?','Load line?','How emitter/source degeneration helps?','Headroom?','What if signal large?']),
L(291,'Amplifiers','What limits amplifier bandwidth?','Bandwidth is limited by device transit and parasitic capacitances/inductances, gain/compensation, source/load impedance, feedback stability, packaging and measurement setup.','Internal poles reduce gain with frequency, while Miller effect and load capacitance can dominate. Gain-bandwidth trade-offs apply to particular compensated amplifiers and small signals; large signals can instead be slew-rate limited. At low frequency, coupling/bypass networks may set corners. A bandwidth number needs gain, amplitude, load, distortion and criterion such as −3 dB.', ['Linear small-signal regime unless stated','Source/load and gain configuration specified'],['Equating slew rate and bandwidth','Quoting bandwidth without gain/load'],['What is pole?','Miller effect?','Gain-bandwidth?','Why capacitive load unstable?','How measure response?']),
L(292,'Environmental Qualification','Why are defence materials tested under extremes?','Testing under representative extremes checks whether required properties, interfaces and failure margins survive actual mechanical, thermal, chemical, electromagnetic and storage conditions.','Temperature cycling, vibration, shock, humidity, corrosion, wear, radiation or other loads are selected from public requirements and failure analysis. Testing only nominal conditions misses coupled degradation; arbitrary “harsh” tests can create irrelevant failures. Qualification uses controlled specimens/configuration, instrumentation, pre/post function, uncertainty and acceptance criteria. Restricted performance details should not be speculated.', ['Public requirement/environment specified','Representative configuration and criteria'],['Harshest test equals best test','Survival equals function'],['Which failure mode?','Why combined loads?','What is margin?','How instrument?','Qualification versus acceptance?']),
L(293,'Sensor Materials','What properties matter in a sensor material?','The target transduction response, sensitivity/selectivity, noise, stability, response time, range, drift, environment, interfaces and reproducibility matter more than one headline property.','A sensing material must couple the measurand to optical, electrical, mechanical, magnetic or chemical output. Defects, morphology, contacts, thickness, temperature and interferents shape response. I would define calibration and detection criteria, test controls/mixtures and ageing, and compare with a reference material. High response without selectivity or stability is not useful.', ['Measurand and transduction mechanism defined','Operating environment/interferents specified'],['Equating large response with good sensor','Ignoring contacts and drift'],['What is selectivity?','Noise source?','How calibrate?','Response time?','How test ageing?']),
L(294,'Experimental Design','How do you design a repeatability test?','Hold defined conditions constant, make independent repeated measurements across relevant time/order, randomize where drift matters and quantify within-condition variance with controls.','I would define measurand, setup, operator, sample position, environment and reset procedure. Repeat scans without reset probe instrument repeatability; removing/replacing sample tests setup; independent preparations test reproducibility and should be reported separately. Control charts or time plots reveal drift. Predeclared exclusions and sufficient repetitions avoid cherry-picking.', ['Repeatability condition explicitly defined','Measurement resolution/stability adequate'],['Calling repeated software reads independent','Mixing batch variation into repeatability silently'],['How many repeats?','How test drift?','Why randomize?','Repeatability versus reproducibility?','What statistic report?']),
L(295,'Reliability','Why use redundancy?','Redundancy can preserve critical function after a fault by providing alternate sensors, paths, processors or modes, but only if detection, switching and common-cause risks are controlled.','Active, standby and diverse redundancy trade mass, power, complexity, dormant failures and recovery time. Fault detection must identify the bad element without switching away from healthy hardware. Shared power, software, environment or design flaws can defeat copies. Graceful degradation, margins and procedures may be better for some functions. Redundancy should follow criticality analysis, not habit.', ['Critical function and failure modes defined','Switching/FDIR verified'],['More copies always safer','Ignoring common cause'],['Active versus cold?','What is voter?','How test backup?','Dormant failure?','When graceful degradation?']),
L(296,'Research Motivation','What astronomy question interests you?','Choose one precise question, name the observable that could answer it, the instrument/data and assumptions, and why the result changes understanding.','A strong response might concern stellar spectra, detector calibration, transients, exoplanet signals or galaxy dynamics, but it must reflect genuine preparation. I would connect model to measurement and uncertainty, mention one current institute group only after official verification, and state the skill gap I am addressing. “How the universe works” is too broad.', ['Genuine topic and basic literature prepared','Relevant group/source current'],['Only childhood fascination','No observable or method'],['What observable?','Which instrument?','Main uncertainty?','Alternative explanation?','Why this group?']),
L(297,'Astronomical Instrumentation','How does your materials background help astronomy?','Materials skills transfer to detector/optics/coating/thermal structures, contamination control and calibrated characterization, while astronomy-specific measurement and data skills still require training.','I would cite actual synthesis or characterization tasks only after they are confirmed. Instrument materials influence band response, dark noise, coatings, stability, outgassing, radiation and thermal distortion. My contribution could begin with controlled material/device measurement and uncertainty, not an unsupported claim of astronomical expertise. The exact fit depends on the institute’s current instrumentation work.', ['Actual project skills known','Current astronomy instrumentation need verified'],['Generic “materials are everywhere”','Claiming project material is telescope-ready'],['Which component?','What property?','How test vacuum/thermal?','What astronomy skill gap?','What first deliverable?']),
L(298,'Galactic Astrophysics','What is an active galactic nucleus?','An AGN is a compact, highly luminous galactic nucleus powered primarily by accretion onto a supermassive black hole, producing radiation and sometimes jets across wavelengths.','Gravitational energy released in an accretion flow can power continuum, broad/narrow emission lines, X-rays and radio jets. Orientation, obscuration, accretion rate, black-hole mass and host environment create different observed classes. Variability constrains source size, but one bright nucleus is not sufficient identification. Spectra, multi-wavelength data and time behavior test models.', ['Accretion-powered framework','Observed band and selection stated'],['Saying luminosity comes from black hole surface','Treating all AGN classes as intrinsically different objects'],['Why variability useful?','What are emission lines?','How jets form?','Orientation unification?','How measure mass?']),
L(299,'Galactic Astrophysics','Why are galaxy spectra useful?','Galaxy spectra reveal redshift, stellar populations, gas excitation, kinematics, dust and chemical evidence through continuum and lines under population/radiative-transfer models.','Absorption features trace integrated stars and velocity dispersion; emission lines probe ionized gas, star formation or nuclear activity; wavelength shifts give radial/cosmological information. Aperture, resolution, calibration, dust, blending and population degeneracy matter. A spectrum is integrated light and does not uniquely decompose every component without spatial or multi-band evidence.', ['Calibrated spectrum and aperture known','Population/line model stated'],['Assigning one line uniquely to mechanism','Ignoring aperture and dust'],['How get redshift?','What is velocity dispersion?','Line-ratio use?','Age-metallicity degeneracy?','Why spatial spectroscopy?']),
L(300,'Cosmology','What is the evidence for expansion?','Distance–redshift observations, time-dilation and multiple cosmological probes are consistent with an expanding spacetime; no single Hubble plot point establishes the model.','Spectral redshift correlated with independently calibrated distance indicators was foundational. Modern evidence includes supernova light-curve time dilation, CMB and baryon acoustic scales, and consistency of structure/age observations within cosmology. Peculiar velocities, calibration and selection affect inference. Expansion is described by scale-factor dynamics, not an explosion from a central point into empty space.', ['FLRW large-scale framework','Distance indicators/redshifts calibrated'],['Calling redshift alone proof of expansion','Treating expansion as local motion from a centre'],['What is Hubble law?','Why low-z peculiar velocity?','Time dilation evidence?','What is scale factor?','Which distance definition?'])]
